latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Remove full-width lines:\n   a. Scan each row and column of the input grid.\n   b. If a row or column consists entirely of the same non-zero color, mark it for removal.\n   c. Create a new grid with the marked rows and columns removed.\n\n2. Identify shape clusters:\n   a. Use a flood fill algorithm to identify connected regions of the same color.\n   b. Store each shape cluster as a list of coordinates and its color.\n   c. Calculate the bounding box (min and max x, y coordinates) for each cluster.\n\n3. Determine relative positions of shape clusters:\n   a. Calculate the center point of the cleaned grid (after line removal).\n   b. For each shape cluster, determine its position relative to this center point (top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right).\n\n4. Create a 6x6 output grid:\n   a. Initialize all cells to 0 (black).\n\n5. Map and scale shape clusters to the output grid:\n   a. Divide the 6x6 output grid into four 3x3 quadrants.\n   b. For each shape cluster:\n      i. Determine which 3x3 quadrant it should map to based on its relative position.\n      ii. Calculate scaling factors:\n          - x_scale = 3 / cluster_width\n          - y_scale = 3 / cluster_height\n          - Use min(x_scale, y_scale) to maintain aspect ratio\n      iii. For each point in the cluster:\n           - new_x = (x - min_x) * scale + quadrant_offset_x\n           - new_y = (y - min_y) * scale + quadrant_offset_y\n           - Round new_x and new_y to nearest integer\n           - If within bounds, set output_grid[new_y][new_x] to the cluster's color\n\n6. Adjust for overlaps and empty spaces:\n   a. If multiple clusters map to the same quadrant, adjust their positions within the 3x3 area to minimize overlap.\n   b. If a quadrant is empty, check if nearby clusters can be partially extended into it to better utilize space.\n\n7. Final cleanup:\n   a. Ensure no shapes are cut off at quadrant boundaries.\n   b. If any isolated single cells exist, consider removing them to clean up the output.\n\n8. Verify output:\n   a. Check that the output grid is 6x6.\n   b. Ensure all significant shapes from the input are represented.\n   c. Verify that the relative positions of shapes are maintained.\n\n9. Create and return the final ColoredGrid object:\n   a. Use the processed 6x6 grid to create a new ColoredGrid object.\n   b. Return this object as the solution.\n\nThis plan addresses the key aspects of the challenge:\n- It removes full-width lines as required.\n- It preserves the main shapes and their relative positions.\n- It adapts to different input sizes and shape distributions.\n- It maintains the color and basic structure of each shape.\n- It produces a consistent 6x6 output regardless of input size.\n\nBy focusing on shape clusters and their relative positions, rather than rigid quadrants, this approach should be more robust and able to handle a variety of input configurations."
